  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Riyas T.K vs Food Inspector on 8 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Friday, The 8Th Day Of January 2016/18Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 3255 of 2006 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 158/2000 Of Additional Sessions Court (Adhoc)-Ii, Thalassery Cc 634/1999 Of The Additional Chief Judicial Magistrate Court, Thalassery Revision Petitioners/Appellants/Accused.: ------------------------------------------ 1. Riyas T.K., S/O.Abdulla, Seepy House, Acharath Road, Thalassery-2. 2. T.K.Khaleel, S/O.Abdulla, Rolex Bakery, 30/372, Saidar Palli Thalassery. By Adv. Sri.Grashious Kuriakose Respondents/Respondents/Complainant & State.: -------------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Tellicherry Municipality, Tellicherry. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. By Public Prosecutor Sri.V.S.Sreejith Riyas T.K vs Food Inspector on 8 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/145304515/ 1 This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 08-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P. No.3255 of 2006 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 8th day of January, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "3. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 13.1.1998 at about 10.30 a.m., PW3, the Food Inspector purchased 750 gram of banana chips in three packets from the first accused. The second accused was the licencee of the shop. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. After analysis, the Public Analyst filed Ext.P19 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for banana chips as per the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1955 and therefore, the same was adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. As per Ext.P7 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory also, the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for the food item and hence the same was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW5 were examined and Exts.P1 to P20 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 and DW2 were examined on the side of the revision petitioners.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P7 certificate cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Riyas T.K vs Food Inspector on 8 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/145304515/ 2 Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P7 certificate cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Riyas T.K vs Food Inspector on 8 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/145304515/ 2 Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
